[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-45", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 45 — IC05 from the memberships through composite lookups: 2.4 s → 161 ms p50", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-45.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 45 - IC05 from the memberships, through composite lookups: 2.4 s -> 161 ms\n\n> Seed: iteration 44's S35. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung :sf1-full;\n> SUT levi (M4 4P+6E, 16 threads, 25.4M quads), driver judah, LAN.\n\n## Generate / Rank\n\n  IC05's cost was its shape: every post of every reached person was a\n  candidate before the join date filtered it. The plan it needs starts\n  from the other end and needs two lookups the datom plane did not\n  offer as one: a person's memberships joined after a date, and a\n  person's posts in a given forum.\n\n  datalog 9d010f97: COMPOSITE LOOKUPS. Two adjacent clauses on a fresh\n  entity -- [?e g ?gv] [?e a ?av], or the range form on a, with ?gv\n  bound -- become one lookup through the group order (the structure the\n  grouped drive added in iteration 35) under gv: a point when av is\n  bound or ground, a cut when it is a range, all entries when free.\n  Bound-ness is tracked in clause order from the inputs; applied only\n  where the drive did not take the query and the db is materialized.\n  Tests: fused vs unfused (adjacency broken by a no-op predicate) on\n  point, bound, ground and range forms, with a hidden quad.\n\n  IC05 rewritten membership-first (no derived-id function clause):\n\n    first parameter (empty answer)   2,456 ms -> 6.2 ms\n    all 99 parameters, in-thread     p50 160.7 ms, p90 380.6, max 2,301; 78 non-empty\n    hand-written twin                ~680 ms; gate 99/99 agree (4,780 checks in all)\n\n  15x on the median parameter; what remains is the answer's size --\n  tens of thousands of (person, forum, post) triples for a mid-timeline\n  minDate.\n\n  Still not admitted to the mix. This driver's mix is uniform round-\n  robin; at 161 ms a 1/12 share of a 4k ops/s mix would need 48\n  thread-seconds per second. LDBC's own mix is not uniform -- complex\n  reads run at per-query frequencies, short reads dominate -- so the\n  honest way to admit IC05 is the official frequency table in the\n  driver (S36), not a share chosen here to make it fit.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - The composite is the second structure that came out of one query's\n    profile and then paid for another: the group order was built for\n    IC02's top-20 and now answers IC05's membership cut and its\n    (creator, forum) posts. The datom plane's \"no composite index\" was\n    an implementation state, not a property (ADR-2809041200).\n  - Two things now block a wider mix, and neither is engine speed: the\n    official operation frequencies (a driver change) and the host.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: no row moved (4631.8 stands); a\nserved op 15x faster with the twin agreeing on every parameter. Ratio\nto the audited No.1: 30.\n\nSeeds for iteration 46, in order:\n\n  - S36: the official LDBC operation frequencies in the driver, then\n    admit IC05.\n  - S34: bindings as arrays inside the join.\n  - Owner: a SUT host (M4; SF3's 13 GB).\n"}]
